A||N||11||fiero651||Motor really dogs when ac is turned on||||13||
Z||000000||fiero651||06-11-2001||02:02 AM||||Hey everyone.  Got an interesting one for ya all.  My 2.8 in my 86 SE dogs down severaly when you turn on the air.  On the hyway the car will feel like it is being held back, in town if you are not careful the car will die at a stop.  Now this only shows up when the air is on.  Off the car has plenty of power on and idles at 1000 rpm roughly.  This engine has 150K on it so I am not surprised just wondering what the hell is going on.||||reg||13
Z||000001||Monkeyman||06-11-2001||05:54 AM||||Mine does the same thing.  I understand that you lose horsepower when you run the A/C, but it's almost funny how much power I lose in my car.  Almost like I hit my brakes.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero651||06-12-2001||12:57 AM||||Well doesn't anyone have any suggestions for me.  It is actually starting to get hot around here finally and I like using my AC<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||06-12-2001||01:48 AM||||I don't really know, but have some guesses. Battery could be low. Alternator might be going down hill. Belt might be on <I>too</I> tight. Those are just guesses so don't laugh at me. Are you pulling up any codes?<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">    <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rob Durham||06-12-2001||12:32 PM||||Does using the power pully help at all??<P>I have the same problem with the power drain and was thinking of getting the power pully.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://rs-designs.com/87GT.html" TARGET=_blank>  <IMG SRC="http://rs-designs.com/Banner.jpg">  </A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||sjp777||06-12-2001||08:18 PM||||Yes, power pulley. Drives the accessories slower but they still will turn fast enough to cool the car and keep the juice running.<P>Engine will pull stronger with the ac going, even better with the ac off.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg"> <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||PBJ||06-12-2001||08:31 PM||||A power pully I think is your only option. If your A/C compressor was siezing and robbing to much power it would not last long.<P>A/C does take alot of engine power espcially noticable from a stop when engine rpm is low and little horse power is being produced.<P>NORMAL-but you gotta love the cool air. <P>I had a Gr Am with a duke with a/c and loved the cool air but WOW, K cars passing me from a stop-hmmm   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||06-12-2001||10:30 PM||||mine dogs down a little too. Didnt older cars have some kind of switch that kicked out the compressor at full throttle? I seem to remember it.<P>If not, shouldnt be hard to rig a momentary contact switch on the throttle linkage, compressor only has 2 wires. would make contact all the time, mounted somewhere solid, and with an arm or something, break contact (turning off compressor clutch power) when you used WOT.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||mbramble||06-13-2001||10:52 AM||||A bit off topic but my wife drives a 94 Geo Metro -- the 3 cylinder jobs.  She loves it and it gets GREAT gas mileage.  But, on hot days, when she has the AC running  -- if she is going up a hill, she reaches over and turns the AC off!!  I always tease her about turning her supercharger on by turning her AC off.  Quite a noticeable difference.||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||06-13-2001||01:58 PM||||You used to be able to buy a vacuum operated switch to cut the compressor out when accelerating. The switch would cut out when the vacuum dropped to a certain point.<P>Haven't seen one of these in quite a while. Some cars had a similar feature in their ECM's to increase acceleration and gas mileage.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dwayne Mohr||06-14-2001||06:50 PM||||  My wifes 89 Grand AM started this accompanied by a A/C belt that squealed a lot and really got chewed up.  It was a seized compressor.  ||||reg||9
Z||000011||theogre||06-14-2001||08:12 PM||||There are several things.....<P>First of all... check the AC pressures. If the high side is over what it should be you'll need more power into the compressor. You'll also tear uip the compressor.<P>A6 compressors run at full output at all times. And A6 is known to be a real hog as it is... Converting to a variable displacement compressor like the V5 would reduce engine load because the V5 adjusts it's output to match system demand. This isn't a simple job but it may be something for the more advanced people to consider. (It's more invollved than just swapping the compressor. You have to make sure all the wiring is right to.)<P>Power pulleys may be an option. Keep in mind this would effect high side pressure and any future testing on the AC system has to allow for that or you could easily over charge the system. Especially with R134...||||reg||1
